  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Soreness and cracking dry skin of the hands, predominantly the palms, present for about 3–4 months and now painful enough to affect work.\n- Symptoms: Severe dryness with fissures/cracks on palms; itching (not constant but present); occasional bleeding when scratched; no fever, nausea, vomiting, bowel or urinary symptoms; general appetite and wellbeing maintained.\n- Onset/ course: Symptoms started a few months ago, gradually worsening; currently limiting ability to work as a hotel housekeeper.\n- Distribution: Palms mainly; dryness noted on legs as milder, non-severe.\n- Aggravating/ alleviating factors: Likely exposure to cleaning products at work with intermittent glove use (recent job change ~4 months ago); symptoms worse with use of cleaning chemicals; tried OTC moisturizers (E45) and antihistamines without relief.\n- Past medical history: Type 2 diabetes mellitus (on metformin).\n- Medications/allergies: Metformin; no known allergies.\n- Family/social history: Works as a housekeeper for a hotel; sometimes does not wear gloves while cleaning; no smoking; drinks a couple pints of alcohol per week; family history notable for diabetes.\n- Vital signs/exam findings (transcript-based): Exam describes palms that are very cracked, dry, and itchy with occasional scratching-induced bleeding; no systemic symptoms reported.\n- Labs/imaging: none mentioned.\n- Plan discussed: Advise use of gloves during cleaning; start topical corticosteroid for inflammation; use stronger emollients (moisturizers) several times daily for 2 weeks; follow-up in 1–2 weeks or sooner if worse or spreading; prescription to be sent to pharmacy.\n",
